  summary: [
    "Biological systems form a hierarchy of subsystems (cell &rarr; tissue &rarr; organ &rarr; organ system &rarr; organism) whose integration produces emergent properties that the individual parts do not have alone, illustrated by the cheetah&rsquo;s integrated adaptations for running down prey.",
    "Organs are integrated in animal bodies by hormonal signalling, nervous signalling, and the transport of materials and energy in the blood (assimilation of digested nutrients, gases and waste products).",
    "The endocrine system sends hormones through the bloodstream to specific target organs; compared with nervous signalling, it acts more slowly but over a longer duration &mdash; e.g. insulin, epinephrine and melatonin.",
    "The brain integrates sensory information, stores memories and sends impulses to effectors, while the spinal cord independently integrates involuntary reflexes and autonomic functions such as sweating, defecation and urination.",
    "A full sensory-to-motor pathway (e.g. writing with a pen) passes from receptor &rarr; sensory neuron &rarr; interneurons (up to the cerebral cortex for conscious perception) &rarr; upper motor neuron &rarr; lower motor neuron &rarr; neuromuscular junction &rarr; muscle contraction, crossing to the opposite side of the body near the medulla.",
    "A reflex arc (receptor &rarr; sensory neuron &rarr; relay neuron &rarr; motor neuron &rarr; effector) produces a fast, fixed, involuntary response such as the pain reflex, while the relay neuron&rsquo;s brain connections still allow sensory awareness and even brain override of the reflex.",
    "The cerebellum (10% of brain volume but 50% of its neurons) fine-tunes posture, balance and precise voluntary movement without initiating it; melatonin from the pineal gland, released more in darkness, regulates circadian rhythms including sleep.",
    "Epinephrine from the adrenal glands prepares the body for vigorous activity (raising heart rate, breathing rate and blood glucose), while the hypothalamus&ndash;pituitary axis forms the body&rsquo;s central hormonal control point, governing growth, reproduction and homeostasis (including storing hypothalamic ADH and oxytocin).",
    "Heart rate and ventilation rate are both controlled by feedback: baroreceptors (blood pressure) and chemoreceptors (CO<sub>2</sub>/pH) send input to control centres in the medulla, which adjust antagonistic sympathetic/parasympathetic output to the SA node and respiratory muscles.",
    "Peristalsis along the gut is controlled, between the voluntary acts of swallowing and defecation, by the involuntary enteric nervous system (ENS), coordinated with the CNS.",
    "<strong>HL:</strong> plant shoots show positive phototropism, growing towards unilateral light; classic decapitation and agar-block experiments show this depends on a diffusible growth substance (auxin) released from the shoot tip, not on light acting directly on the stem.",
    "<strong>HL:</strong> auxin is transported polarly (passive influx, active ATP-driven efflux via auxin efflux carriers) and promotes cell elongation via the acid growth hypothesis; its antagonistic, interdependent interaction with cytokinin integrates root and shoot growth, while ethylene drives fruit ripening through positive feedback."
  ]
